---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import UnlockPdf from '../islands/UnlockPdf';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Can PDFSherpa unlock a PDF if I forgot the password?', a: 'No. PDFSherpa never guesses, cracks or bypasses passwords. You need the password that opens the file. If you’ve lost it, ask the person or organisation that sent the PDF for a new copy.' },
  { q: 'Why do banks and companies send password-protected PDFs?', a: 'To protect statements and payslips in transit. The password is often a mix of your name, date of birth or account details, and it’s explained in the email that came with the file.' },
  { q: 'What about PDFs that open fine but can’t be edited or printed?', a: 'Those have owner “permission restrictions” rather than an open password. PDFSherpa doesn’t remove restrictions without the owner’s password. Ask the document’s owner for an unrestricted copy.' },
  { q: 'Is my password sent anywhere?', a: 'No. The password and the PDF stay in your browser. Unlocking runs locally with pdf.js and a WebAssembly build of Ghostscript.' },
  { q: 'Will the quality change?', a: 'No. Images are passed through and text stays selectable. The file is only rewritten without encryption.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout
  slug="unlock-pdf"
  title="Unlock PDF – Remove a Password You Know, Free | PDFSherpa"
  description="Remove the password from a PDF you can already open, so you don’t have to type it every time. Free and private: no upload, no password cracking."
  h1="Unlock PDF"
  lead="Remove the open password from a PDF you already have the password for, like a bank statement or payslip, so it opens without asking. Nothing is uploaded."
  howToTitle="How to Remove a PDF Password"
  steps={['Select your password-protected PDF.', 'Type the password that opens it.', 'Click <strong>Unlock PDF</strong> and download the unprotected copy.']}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={['protect-pdf', 'compress-pdf', 'merge-pdf', 'pdf-to-text']}
>
  <UnlockPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>What Unlock PDF does</h2>
    <p>
      Many PDFs arrive locked with an open password: bank statements, credit-card bills, payslips, insurance documents. That’s good for security in transit,
      but annoying when you have to type the password every time, or when an upload portal or another tool can’t open the file. Unlock PDF makes a copy of
      the document without the password, <strong>only after you enter the correct password yourself</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Open the protected PDF.</strong> PDFSherpa checks whether it really needs a password.</li>
      <li><strong>Type the password.</strong> It’s checked locally first. A wrong password gets a clear message, never a broken file.</li>
      <li><strong>Download.</strong> The unlocked copy keeps the original name and quality.</li>
    </ol>

    <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="tool-article" />

    <h2>Typical uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Unlock a bank statement before uploading it to a visa or loan application that rejects protected PDFs.</li>
      <li>Unlock several monthly statements, then combine them with <a href="/merge-pdf">Merge PDF</a>.</li>
      <li>Remove the password before <a href="/compress-pdf">compressing</a> or <a href="/grayscale-pdf">converting to grayscale</a>, since most tools can’t edit locked files.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it won’t do</h2>
    <p>
      PDFSherpa doesn’t crack, guess or bypass passwords, and it doesn’t strip owner permission restrictions without the owner’s password. If a PDF
      isn’t yours to unlock, ask its owner. To add a password back later, use <a href="/protect-pdf">Protect PDF</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      An online tool that asks for a password should never send it to a server. PDFSherpa keeps both the PDF and the password inside your browser
      and forgets them when you close the tab. The unlocked copy can be read by anyone who gets it, so store it carefully.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
